      "side": 1,
      "message": "Short answer: Remove `Ok(())`, remove the semicolon in the unsafe block, everything will work without disabling the lint.\n\nLong answer:\n`!` as a *general type* is unstable. (This is what lets you do e.g. `Result\u003c(), !\u003e` or similar). `!` specifically as a return type will work fine. This is why this situation was able to happen - bindgen is putting `!` in the return position of `AVmPayload_runVsockRpcServer`. So `fn foo() -\u003e ! { panic!(\"oh no\") }` is totally legal, `fn foo() -\u003e Result\u003c!\u003e {Ok(panic!(\"oh no\"))}` still needs the unstable feature.\n\nHowever, you don\u0027t need to put this in the type - because `!` is a divergent type, it implicitly coerces to any other type you want,  `Result\u003c()\u003e` included. This is why removing the semi and the Ok(()) works - you return the `!` \"value\" that comes out of a function that can\u0027t finish, and so it matches any type.",
